WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.110
GPU offload moves model layers or operations from CPU execution to an accelerator.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:10.119
Full offload is usually fastest when the model and cache fit in VRAM.

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:13.202
Partial offload can make a larger model usable,

00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:17.441
but each remaining CPU stage and data transfer can reduce speed.

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:22.836
Increase offload while watching VRAM headroom and stability rather than setting the maximum blindly.

00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:26.690
Batch settings affect how much prompt work is processed together.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:31.314
Larger batches may improve accelerator utilization and prompt throughput but consume more

00:00:31.314 --> 00:00:33.241
memory and can increase latency.

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:35.167
Thread count affects CPU-side work;

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.792
using every logical thread is not always fastest because memory bandwidth, scheduling,

00:00:39.792 --> 00:00:41.333
and background tasks matter.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:44.416
Test a small range around the runtime default.

00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:49.040
Select the backend that is genuinely supported by the hardware and build.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.967
CUDA commonly accelerates NVIDIA devices.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:54.821
ROCm supports compatible AMD configurations, Vulkan can provide cross-vendor acceleration,

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.289
Metal supports Apple platforms, and CPU fallback preserves portability.

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:01.372
Confirm the active backend in logs and utilization.

00:01:01.372 --> 00:01:05.611
A successful launch may still be running mostly on the CPU.

00:01:05.611 --> 00:01:09.079
Defaults aim for broad compatibility, not every machine's optimum.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:13.318
Context length, cache format, offload, batch, threads, and memory mapping interact.

00:01:13.318 --> 00:01:17.171
Record a baseline, change one setting, rerun the same workload,

00:01:17.171 --> 00:01:20.640
and keep only improvements that preserve quality and reliability.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.952
Tuning should reduce a measured constraint,

00:01:22.952 --> 00:01:26.420
not create a mysterious collection of copied command-line flags.
